            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Gives the pseudo-inverse of a matrix: the nearest thing to an inverse that a matrix has, even when it has no true inverse.\n\nOnly a square matrix with no redundancy can be inverted (np.linalg.inv). Most matrices that turn up in practice are not like that: more equations than unknowns (ten measurements of three values), fewer, or rows that repeat each other. The pseudo-inverse still undoes as much of the matrix as can be undone, and where an exact answer is impossible it gives the closest one.\n\nMultiplying it by b gives the least-squares solution of A x = b - the x that comes closest to satisfying every equation at once - which is the heart of fitting a line or a plane to measurements, calibrating a sensor against reference readings, or solving for joint angles that best reach a target. For a square matrix that does have an inverse, it is exactly that inverse.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.linalg.pinv\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ninput:\nThe matrix: any 2-D array, of any shape. Every input is inverted and sent.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\npseudo-inverse:\nThe pseudo-inverse. An m x n matrix gives an n x m one.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA is 3 x 2: three rows, two columns - more equations than unknowns, so it has no inverse. Its pseudo-inverse is 2 x 3, and multiplied by A it gives the 2 x 2 identity (1 0 / 0 1): it undoes A completely from that side. (A times its pseudo-inverse is NOT the 3 x 3 identity - three rows cannot all be recovered from two numbers.)\n\nRELATED:\nnp.linalg.inv is the exact inverse of a square matrix. np.linalg.lstsq solves A x = b by least squares directly, and also says how well. np.matmul multiplies matrices.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1000,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Gives the pseudo-inverse of a matrix: the nearest thing to an inverse that a matrix has, even when it has no true inverse.\n\nOnly a square matrix with no redundancy can be inverted (np.linalg.inv). Most matrices that turn up in practice are not like that: more equations than unknowns (ten measurements of three values), fewer, or rows that repeat each other. The pseudo-inverse still undoes as much of the matrix as can be undone, and where an exact answer is impossible it gives the closest one.\n\nMultiplying it by b gives the least-squares solution of A x = b - the x that comes closest to satisfying every equation at once - which is the heart of fitting a line or a plane to measurements, calibrating a sensor against reference readings, or solving for joint angles that best reach a target. For a square matrix that does have an inverse, it is exactly that inverse.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.linalg.pinv\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ninput:\nThe matrix: any 2-D array, of any shape. Every input is inverted and sent.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\npseudo-inverse:\nThe pseudo-inverse. An m x n matrix gives an n x m one.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA is 3 x 2: three rows, two columns - more equations than unknowns, so it has no inverse. Its pseudo-inverse is 2 x 3, and multiplied by A it gives the 2 x 2 identity (1 0 / 0 1): it undoes A completely from that side. (A times its pseudo-inverse is NOT the 3 x 3 identity - three rows cannot all be recovered from two numbers.)\n\nRELATED:\nnp.linalg.inv is the exact inverse of a square matrix. np.linalg.lstsq solves A x = b by least squares directly, and also says how well. np.matmul multiplies matrices."
        },
